1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
The payment tool says ok.
Did the payment happen?

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Same request, same reply,
different realities.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Left: a program that trusts the reply.
Write, see ok, report done.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Right: the trained agent, live.
It reads the payment back, finds none,
and pays before it reports.

5
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:56,000
The reveal: one payment for the trained agent,
none for the script.

6
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Every verdict is read from the database,
not from the reply. Each run can be downloaded.

7
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:24,000
On 440 locked tasks, trusting the reply
falsely completes 0.864 of the time.

8
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Recovery demonstrations then GRPO:
0.513 to 0.819 on 450 frozen tasks, three seeds.

9
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:45,000
False completion 0.121; far fewer duplicates than bounded retry.
The retry test fails, and we say so.

10
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:53,000
The hand-written script still beats every learned agent.
Our review: a read-free same-key policy gets close on completion.

11
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Registered before training: teach hand-off.
Locked test: 0.972 against 0.002 for a matched control.

12
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:10,000
But a same-key resend could have settled those cases.
Where hand-off is the only safe action: about four in ten, seed-fragile.

13
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Nothing lost on resolvable work.
We report all of it.

14
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Projected: trusting the reply costs about ten times
the trained agent's exposure; the script cuts it further.

15
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:37,000
A simulator: the dollars are a projection, not savings.
Every number has one file and one command.

16
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Transaction-aware recovery under ambiguous execution.
Not a new RL optimizer, not a claim of safety.
